[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186064174 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-41aba970-483d-56bd-ae8a-765c80a69d27" "question-7a7beadf-3b71-5ed6-8cee-74b01fe2145a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-41aba970-483d-56bd-ae8a-765c80a69d27" :question/id #uuid "41aba970-483d-56bd-ae8a-765c80a69d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-28887" :question/problem "Find the intersection of intervals $(-2,\\infty )\\cap (1,\\infty ).$ Express the result as an inequality." :question/worked-solution "We start by sketching the two intervals $(-2,\\infty )$ and $(1,\\infty )$ separately:\n\n![](images/4b/4bd70e5f89643a5d2b19954003435b4c6aeec24c68bb3cd1f6a4e7a98c14f847.png)\n\n![](images/ef/eff58d0bb2d496d608ae1e415f38589c5b9b13efe1f282d172fb5313ba6792ff.png)\n\nThe intersection $(-2,\\infty )\\cap (1,\\infty )$ represents the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/61/6131e16f37e8ec44eae8c085be60a28dd094390e1975bdafe256cdc9e229f3dc.png)\n\nThus, $(-2,\\infty )\\cap (1,\\infty )=(1,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the inequality $x>1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c997497d-11b1-5f1f-b96d-9d3ba0bd5017"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c997497d-11b1-5f1f-b96d-9d3ba0bd5017" :answer-field/id #uuid "c997497d-11b1-5f1f-b96d-9d3ba0bd5017" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-648a569e-d6e2-581e-aaf0-c3843ce4953c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ad77b642-5d0e-5f8c-a29c-a1e7f5ae4898" "answer-9ab7a7d4-8d57-556e-a809-860ec8ff4839" "answer-dfd54e2f-92b2-5044-821d-60b7f0fbe85a" "answer-648a569e-d6e2-581e-aaf0-c3843ce4953c" "answer-e5b8e284-2962-5a81-950b-945c41e9dbf2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad77b642-5d0e-5f8c-a29c-a1e7f5ae4898" :answer/id #uuid "ad77b642-5d0e-5f8c-a29c-a1e7f5ae4898" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2<x<1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ab7a7d4-8d57-556e-a809-860ec8ff4839" :answer/id #uuid "9ab7a7d4-8d57-556e-a809-860ec8ff4839" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2<x\\le 1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dfd54e2f-92b2-5044-821d-60b7f0fbe85a" :answer/id #uuid "dfd54e2f-92b2-5044-821d-60b7f0fbe85a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x<2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-648a569e-d6e2-581e-aaf0-c3843ce4953c" :answer/id #uuid "648a569e-d6e2-581e-aaf0-c3843ce4953c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x>1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5b8e284-2962-5a81-950b-945c41e9dbf2" :answer/id #uuid "e5b8e284-2962-5a81-950b-945c41e9dbf2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ge 1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064175 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-03c05eb1-a1b5-5135-a57f-9c96b45d2734"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-03c05eb1-a1b5-5135-a57f-9c96b45d2734" :question/id #uuid "03c05eb1-a1b5-5135-a57f-9c96b45d2734" :question/math-academy-id "q-44840" :question/problem "Given the inequalities $-1<x<4$ and $x>2$, express their common set of solutions as an interval." :question/worked-solution "We start by sketching the solutions to the two inequalities separately:\n\n![](images/f9/f9409f298a0f4d1fd06bdac159e8fd7eb034ee577d2c8956730f0cc3919e051a.png)\n\n![](images/f4/f4fd2c61f600408511d967690cf02c95a3d2fa470aa5d7e9176a222d16823d5b.png)\n\nThe common set of values consists of the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/fd/fdf3410c69cf8559abfb7b223fc175bd1704d6334c8abbceb45240c894514172.png)\n\nTherefore, the common set of values is\n$(2,4).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0841ef22-9728-5f83-9df1-d7f2695e1b84"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0841ef22-9728-